# Analyzers

Reactor ships a set of Roslyn analyzers that catch common mistakes while
you write code: a hook inside an `if`, a list item with no key, a hard-coded
color, an icon button with no name for screen readers. Each rule has an ID
that starts with `REACTOR`. Many rules also have a code fix that rewrites
the code for you.

You don't install anything extra. The analyzers are inside the
`Microsoft.UI.Reactor` NuGet package, in its `analyzers/dotnet/cs` folder,
so every project that references the package runs them.

## Where the warnings show up

![Your code is checked by the REACTOR analyzers. The results show up in three places: the editor, the dotnet build output, and mur check.](images/analyzers/two-surfaces.svg)

- **In the editor.** Visual Studio and VS Code with the C# extension show
  a squiggle while you type. Press `Ctrl+.` on the squiggle to see the code
  fix, if the rule has one.
- **In the build.** `dotnet build` and CI print the same warnings and
  errors.
- **In `mur check`.** It runs the build and prints each diagnostic on one
  line, with a hint on how to fix it. See [`mur check`](#mur-check).

## All rules

The table lists every rule in the analyzer package. The **Fix** column shows
which rules have a code fix. Select an ID to read more about it.

| ID | Category | Severity | Fix | What it catches |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| [REACTOR0050](#reactor0050) | Reactor.Descriptor | Warning | | An `Optional<T>` OneWay descriptor entry has no `dp:`, so `Unset` can't clear the value. |
| [REACTOR_A11Y_001](#reactor_a11y_001) | Microsoft.UI.Reactor.Accessibility | Warning | | An icon-only button has no accessible name. |
| [REACTOR_A11Y_002](#reactor_a11y_002) | Microsoft.UI.Reactor.Accessibility | Warning | | An image has no alt text and is not marked as decorative. |
